Here is the commit message:
bpf: sockmap, updating the sg structure should also update
 curr

Curr pointer should be updated when the sg structure is shifted.

Fixes: 7246d8ed4dcce ("bpf: helper to pop data from messages")
Signed-off-by: John Fastabend <john.fastabend@gmail.com>
Link: https://lore.kernel.org/r/20231206232706.374377-3-john.fastabend@gmail.com
Signed-off-by: Jakub Kicinski <kuba@kernel.org>

Here is the root cause:
The bug arises from the failure to update the `curr` pointer in the `sg` structure of the `sk_msg` when the structure is modified (e.g., during data push, pull, or pop operations). This can lead to inconsistencies in the state of the message, as the `curr` pointer may not accurately reflect the current position in the scatter-gather list, potentially causing incorrect data handling or access.

Here is the patch:

+static void sk_msg_reset_curr(struct sk_msg *msg)
+{
+	u32 i = msg->sg.start;
+	u32 len = 0;
+
+	do {
+		len += sk_msg_elem(msg, i)->length;
+		sk_msg_iter_var_next(i);
+		if (len >= msg->sg.size)
+			break;
+	} while (i != msg->sg.end);
+
+	msg->sg.curr = i;
+	msg->sg.copybreak = 0;
+}
+
 static const struct bpf_func_proto bpf_msg_cork_bytes_proto = {
 	.func           = bpf_msg_cork_bytes,
 		      msg->sg.end - shift;
 out:
+	sk_msg_reset_curr(msg);
 	msg->data = sg_virt(&msg->sg.data[first_sge]) + start - offset;
 	msg->data_end = msg->data + bytes;
 	}

+	sk_msg_reset_curr(msg);
 	sk_msg_compute_data_pointers(msg);
 	return 0;
 	sk_mem_uncharge(msg->sk, len - pop);
 	msg->sg.size -= (len - pop);
+	sk_msg_reset_curr(msg);
 	sk_msg_compute_data_pointers(msg);
 	return 0;


